== Introduction ==

'''''Parts''''' are a core element of how creatures, objects, zones, and more are implemented in the game. In essence, a "part" can be thought of as a bit of code attached to an object, consisting primarily of

* some data for that object, and
* some [[Modding:Events|event handlers]].

Many important game elements are implemented using parts. Some examples include:

* The [[look]] description of objects is implemented using the <code>Description</code> part{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=Description}}.
* The tile used to render objects is implemented using the <code>Render</code> part{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=Render}}.
* Mutations (and bonuses to mutations) are stored in the <code>Mutations</code> part{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=Mutations}}, and individual mutations are also parts in and of themselves.
* The player's score in [[A Call to Arms]] is implemented as a zone part{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=XRL.World.ZoneParts|class=CallToArmsScore}}.
* The code used to track the start of the quest [[Reclamation]] is implemented as a part that gets added to the player{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=OmonporchBattlePart}}.

The [[Modding:Tutorial - Snapjaw Mages|Snapjaw Mages]] tutorial introduces many important parts, and tables with other common parts for objects can be found in [[Modding:Objects]].

== Types of parts ==

Over time, many different types of parts have been implemented to support different kinds of functionality. The following (non-exhaustive) list specifies some of the more common and useful parts that the game uses:

* <code>IPart</code>{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World|class=IPart}}: this is the most generic kind of part that can be added to an object.
** <code>BaseMutation</code>: a part used to represent a single mutation.
** <code>IActivePart</code>{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=IActivePart}}: used to represent [[Modding:Active Parts|active parts]]. This encompasses parts that are only activated under certain conditions to perform various behaviors.
** <code>IGrenade</code>{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=IGrenade}}: specialized part used to enable [[grenade]] behaviors (either when throwing or manually detonating grenades).
** <code>IModification</code>{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=IModification}}: generic part that is used to implement [[item mods]].
** <code>IPlayerPart</code>{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=IPlayerPart}}: a part that follows the player around even after changing bodies. This is usually used to implement player-specific behavior that is independent of the body that the player is in.
** <code>IPoweredPart</code>{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.Parts|class=IPoweredPart}}: a more specific variant of <code>IActivePart</code> which is generally used to represent parts for objects and abilities that require [[power]], e.g. via [[energy cells]].
** <code>IScribedPart</code>{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World|class=IScribedPart}}: a special type of part, primarily intended to be used by modders, that makes it easier to add or remove fields between mod versions. See [[Modding:Serialization (Saving/Loading)]] for more details.
* <code>IZonePart</code>{{Code Reference|namespace=XRL.World.ZoneParts|class=IZonePart}}: a part that can be applied to a [[zone]]. These are often used to help keep track of zone-wide events.

== Examples ==

A part can be added to an object by adding the <code><part/></code> XML tag to it. For instance, the following code would merge the <code>MyPart</code> part into {{favilink|snapjaw scavenger|plural}}:

<syntaxhighlight lang="xml">
<objects>
  <object Name="Snapjaw Scavenger" Load="Merge">
    <part Name="MyPart" />
  </object>
</objects>
</syntaxhighlight>

The same tag can be used in <code>Worlds.xml</code> to add a part to a zone. For example, the sultan tombs in the [[Tomb of the Eaters]] use the <code>AmbientStabilization</code> part to provide ambient [[normality]]:

<syntaxhighlight lang="xml">
<zone Level="5" x="0" y="2" Name="Tomb of *Sultan1Name*" NameContext="the Tomb of the Eaters" DisableForcedConnections="Yes">
  <map FileName="Sultan1SW.rpm"></map>
  <builder Class="FlagInside"></builder>
  <music Track="Music/Deeper Eaters" />
  <part Name="AmbientStabilization" Strength="40" />
</zone>
</syntaxhighlight>

Here is an example of a relatively simple part, implemented in C#. This part does not do anything by itself, it just contains a field <code>Foo</code>:

<syntaxhighlight lang="csharp">
using System;

namespace XRL.World.Parts
{
    // Almost all parts will want to have [Serializable] attached to
    // them, because that will allow the game to convert the part
    // to data when saving the game. In some rare circumstances, you
    // can mark a part [NonSerialized] to make sure that the game
    // _does not_ save the part.
    [Serializable]
    public class MyPart : IPart
    {
        public string Foo = "hello, world!";
    }
}
</syntaxhighlight>

This part could be attached to an object by adding <code><part Name="MyPart"/></code> to the object. If you wanted to specify a custom value for <code>Foo</code> for the part, then you could do something like

<syntaxhighlight lang="xml">
<part Name="MyPart" Foo="goodbye!" />
</syntaxhighlight>

By itself this part is not very useful. The game could hypothetically access and use the <code>Foo</code> data attached to it with some code like

<syntaxhighlight lang="csharp">
if (gameObject.TryGetPart<MyPart>(out var part))
{
    // Do something with part.Foo
}
</syntaxhighlight>

However, this is a use case for which you would typically attach a <code><tag/></code>, <code><property/></code>, or an <code><intproperty/></code> to an object.

So in most cases, you will want your part to also include an event handler. Here is another version of <code>MyPart</code> which prepends the value of <code>Foo</code> to an object's display name:

<syntaxhighlight lang="csharp">
using System;

namespace XRL.World.Parts
{
    // Almost all parts will want to have [Serializable] attached to
    // them, because that will allow the game to convert the part
    // to data when saving the game. In some rare circumstances, you
    // can mark a part [NonSerialized] to make sure that the game
    // _does not_ save the part.
    [Serializable]
    public class MyPart : IPart
    {
        public string Foo = "hello, world!";

        public override bool WantEvent(int ID, int cascade)
        {
            return ID == GetShortDescriptionEvent.ID
                || base.WantEvent(ID, cascade);
        }

        public override bool HandleEvent(GetShortDescriptionEvent E)
        {
            E.AddMark(Foo, DescriptionBuilder.ORDER_ADJUST_EXTREMELY_EARLY);
            return base.HandleEvent(E);
        }
    }
}
</syntaxhighlight>

Now our <code>MyPart</code> part will actually ''do'' something with the data <code>Foo</code> that it comes with. In particular, it will listen for the <code>GetShortDescriptionEvent</code> event. When that event occurs (typically to get a creature or object's name), it will prepend the value of <code>Foo</code> to the name.

See [[Modding:Events]] for more details.

